CREATED:20260512T124742Z
DESCRIPTION:Experience pieces of a live documentary blending music\, film\
 , and food\, followed by a conversation with its creators.\n\n**Free! and
  open to all\, but please register at https://rsvp.duke.edu/M1n0zw.**\n\n
 When our most sacred recipes tell inconvenient truths\, do we have the co
 urage to listen? For Justin Robinson (Carolina Chocolate Drops)\, a famil
 y treat called "stickies" turns out to be stückene\, the German word for 
 scraps. A lifetime of digestive trouble fades after a week of eating West
  African food. A DNA test reveals a German ancestor in a Black Southern f
 amily tree. \n\nRobinson\, a Grammy-winning musician and ethnobotanist\, 
 has spent years pulling at these threads\, following them from his grandp
 arents' kitchen in South Carolina to Nigeria\, to Germany\, and into deep
 er questions about identity\, ancestry\, and the body.\n\n"German Soul\,"
  executive produced by Rhiannon Giddens\, is a "live documentary" that pu
 ts Robinson on stage with film\, original music\, and "taste cues" prepar
 ed by Chef Adé Carrena. The project is being workshopped with the communi
 ties closest to its findings\, to stay in active relationship with the cu
 lture it examines.\n\nFollowing the live documentary portion\, Robinson w
 ill join co-director/producer D.L. Anderson (Vittles Films) for a convers
 ation about how they arrived at this format and what it takes to build it
 . They'll cover the design of a documentary meant to turn over big\, pers
 onal questions in a live room\, the ethics of workshopping hard history w
 ith implicated audiences\, and whether this format can chart a sustainabl
 e path for independent nonfiction.\n\nFree and open to the public. Presen
 ted by Duke CDS Continuing Education. \n\nLearn more about the project: h
 ttps://www.vittles.us/germansoul
DURATION:PT2H
DTSTAMP:20260612T154207Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260617T180000
LAST-MODIFIED:20260612T154207Z
